#
# Remote stim2 configuration.
#
# ESS_RMT_HOST IS NO LONGER SET HERE. Where stim2 listens is a DECLARED
# setting, in local/rig.tcl:
#
#     setting stim host 192.168.88.50      ;# or localhost, the default
#
# or, without editing anything by hand:
#
#     essctrl -c 'settings::put stim host 192.168.88.50 -persist'
#
# config/rig_settings.tcl exports it to ESS_RMT_HOST before any subprocess
# starts, so stimconf.tcl, ess and the ess/ipaddr derivation below all read
# exactly what they always did. A put also reaches the running stim and ess
# interps, and the value is visible as settings/stim/host with its schema,
# which is what lets a page offer it. Validation rejects a URL, whitespace
# and empty at the door rather than once per stimSend.
#
# A rig that still has a local/pre-remote.tcl setting ESS_RMT_HOST is not
# broken: on the first boot after the upgrade that value is ADOPTED into
# local/rig.tcl, and from then on the declaration wins and the line here does
# nothing. dserv says so at boot ("local/pre-remote.tcl is superseded ...").
# Delete the line once you have seen it; keep the file only if you need
# ESS_IPADDR below.
#
# Copy to local/pre-remote.tcl only if you need to override ESS_IPADDR.
#

# The address stim2 should use to reach THIS box.
#
# ESS hands it to stim2 over rmt -- `rmtSend "set dservhost ..."` in
# lib/ess-2.0.tm -- so stim2 knows where to send its datapoints BACK. Getting
# it wrong fails SILENTLY in both directions: stim2 connects to the wrong
# host (often itself) and the datapoints simply never arrive.
#
# DERIVED, not declared -- this is an override, and usually unnecessary.
# dsconf.tcl works it out:
#   1. this setting, if present                            <- always wins
#   2. else, if `setting stim host` names another machine,
#      this box's LAN IPv4 (same value as system/hostaddr)
#   3. else 127.0.0.1 (local stim2; survives a downed link)
#
# So on a normal single-NIC rig you need no file at all and rule 2 does the
# right thing. Set it explicitly when the derived answer would be wrong:
#
#   - multi-homed box, and stim2 is not on the default-route interface
#     (the derivation uses `ip route get`, i.e. the default route)
#   - dserv reachable via NAT/port-forward, or a name rather than an address
#   - you want stim2 pinned to a specific interface
#
# It stays a file rather than a setting because the derivation beats what a
# person will type; a knob would mostly be an opportunity to get it wrong.
#
# Check what was derived with:  dservGet ess/ipaddr
#
# NOTE: the derivation runs at BOOT. Changing `setting stim host` on a
# multi-homed rig can leave ess/ipaddr stale until a restart.
#
set env(ESS_IPADDR)   192.168.88.29
